            "rate_citation": {
              "section": "Ministry of Labour and Employment, reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 586, 24 July 2023",
              "source": "https://www.labour.gov.in/static/uploads/2025/06/93561f36d5094b0cfe52570b4e8d2dc1.pdf",
              "retrieved": "2026-09-05",
              "rules_key": "groups.epf.employee_rate",
              "note": "Read this before quoting the figure. Section 6 of the Employees' Provident Funds and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1952 says the contribution is TEN per cent; its first proviso substitutes 12 per cent only for establishments the Central Government has notified, and that notification could not be retrieved -- epfindia.gov.in and epfo.gov.in refuse requests from this environment. So 0.12 is not the rate read off the section. It is the rate the Government states is applied: an employee of a covered establishment \"is statutorily required to join the fund and to contribute 12% of wages\". Re-source it from the notification when that becomes reachable, and until then treat any figure computed from it as resting on this statement rather than on the Act."
            },
            "wage_citation": {
              "section": "Employees' Provident Funds and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1952, sections 2(b) and 6",
              "source": "https://www.labour.gov.in/static/uploads/2025/06/03cab9b7485af64f747d8c658cb56a7e.pdf",
              "retrieved": "2026-09-05",
              "rules_key": "groups.epf.wage_components",
              "note": "Section 6 computes the contribution on \"basic wages, dearness allowance and retaining allowance\", and section 2(b)(ii) excludes house rent allowance from basic wages by name, along with overtime, bonus and commission. Of the catalogue's entries only `basic` is inside that base; this file carries no dearness or retaining allowance entry yet, and a ticket adding one must add it here too. Whether a \"special allowance\" falls inside section 2(b) has been litigated and is not settled by the section's own words, so it is left out rather than guessed at."
            },
